---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';

/** The loop, start to finish. Judgment is not a stage — it runs across all five. */
const loop = [
  ['Frame the intent', 'Say what a good result has to do'],
  ['Generate working options', 'Build them, do not describe them'],
  ['Review in context', 'Put running software in front of the people who know different parts of the problem'],
  ['Decide together', 'Rank, choose, and say why'],
  ['Strengthen the system', 'Fold the decision back into what everything else is generated from'],
];

/**
 * What a brief has to carry before anything is generated.
 *
 * Each slot holds the question it answers. The section used to ask seven
 * questions in a list, then name six slots, then close by naming seven
 * concepts — three passes over the same idea, none of which was the artifact.
 * The brief is the artifact, so the questions live inside it.
 */
const brief = [
  ['Intent', 'Who is trying to do what, and which decision should get easier?'],
  ['Required behavior', 'What has to remain true across every state, transition, permission and failure path?'],
  ['System constraints', 'Which tokens, components and patterns must the work resolve to?'],
  ['Prohibited actions', 'What may the agent not do without a person?'],
  ['Open questions', 'What should the work help us resolve?'],
  ['Evaluation criteria', 'What would make this wrong even if it looked finished?'],
];

/** Design intent becomes an interface through the system, not around it. */
const encode = [
  ['Design intent', 'The behavior and hierarchy the work has to produce'],
  ['Tokens', 'Colour, type, spacing, radius — the approved values'],
  ['Components', 'The patterns already agreed, and their documented states'],
  ['Behavioral rules', 'Accessibility minimums and brand rules, as constraints rather than notes'],
  ['Working interface', 'Generated inside all four, not compared against them afterwards'],
];

/** Six lenses, one question each. */
const lenses = [
  ['Intent', 'Does this solve the problem we agreed to solve?'],
  ['Behavior', 'Do the states, transitions, permissions and failure paths make sense?'],
  ['System', 'Does this strengthen the product’s patterns, or erode them?'],
  ['Content', 'Does the interface explain what happened and what to do next?'],
  ['Accessibility', 'Can people perceive, understand and operate it in real conditions?'],
  ['Implementation', 'Is the behavior credible in the actual product environment?'],
];

/**
 * Only the boundaries a design leader sets. "Do not deploy to production" and
 * "do not delete data" were here too; anyone who has run an agent for a week
 * sets those, and twelve bullets spent the page's longest list on its least
 * differentiated idea.
 */
const boundaries = [
  ['Invent design-system values', 'If the system has no approved value, the agent names the gap. It does not fill it.'],
  ['Resolve product ambiguity', 'An assumption made at speed is still an assumption, and it arrives looking finished.'],
  ['Treat valid as approved', 'Passing every check is not the same as being the right thing to build.'],
];

/** Where leverage comes from now. */
const leverage = [
  'The clarity of the product intent',
  'The strength of the design system',
  'The quality of the evaluation criteria',
  'The boundaries around autonomous action',
  'The speed and honesty of the review loop',
  'The team’s ability to turn individual learning into shared infrastructure',
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I build the system that AI designs within"
  metaTitle="I build the system that AI designs within | Tyler Young"
  description="An agent can build a screen in minutes. Knowing whether it is the right screen still takes a person who understands the problem. The design systems, constraints and review loops I build for agents to work inside."
  eyebrow="How I design with AI"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Frame · direct · evaluate · align'],
    ['Output', 'Working options, production-ready systems, clearer decisions'],
    ['Principle', 'The machine increases range. People remain responsible for meaning and quality.'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    An agent can build a screen in minutes. Knowing whether it is the right
    screen still takes a person who understands the problem. I build the design
    systems, constraints and review loops that agents work inside, and I am
    accountable for what ships.
  </p>

  <p class="sr-only">
    The operating loop runs in five stages. Frame the intent: define what a good
    result has to do. Generate working options: build them rather than
    describe them. Review in context: put running software in front of the people
    who know different parts of the problem. Decide together: rank, choose, and
    say why. Strengthen the system: fold the decision back into what everything
    else is generated from.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      loop.map(([actor, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{actor}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p class="note-line">Human judgment runs across all five, not at the end.</p>

  <p>
    Turning an idea into something a team could use once took weeks. Now I can
    build several working directions in hours and let the team compare real
    behavior instead of imagined outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The work may move faster now, but my responsibility remains the same:
    understand the problem, decide how the thing should behave, keep the product
    coherent, get the right people into the decision, and notice when a
    good-looking answer is solving the wrong thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A rule I set — this state has to exist, this colour comes from the token
    page, this action needs a person — once got written down and checked later by
    whoever built it. Now it is applied while the thing is being built, so fewer
    of my decisions survive as notes and more survive as behavior.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the work moved upstream of the screen: the brief, the system, the loop,
    and the perimeter around what an agent decides alone.
  </p>
  <h2>I write the brief first</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can build a polished version of a poorly framed idea remarkably
    quickly. So before generating anything, I write a brief that answers six
    questions.
  </p>
  <ol class="loop">
    {
      brief.map(([label, question]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{question}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>That is more specification than a screen drawing carries.</p>
  <h2>The design system moved into the build</h2>
  <p>
    Every colour, type style, spacing value, radius, interaction pattern and
    accessibility rule should resolve to the system. If a required value or
    pattern does not exist, the agent should name the gap rather than quietly
    invent an exception.
  </p>
  <p>
    That puts the design system somewhere new. It is no longer a document people
    consult or a standard applied at review — it is part of the environment the
    interface comes out of.
  </p>

  <p class="sr-only">
    Design intent, tokens, components and behavioral rules together produce the
    working interface: the interface is generated inside all four rather than
    compared against them afterwards.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop" aria-hidden="true">
    {
      encode.map(([actor, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{actor}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    At Atheer I rebuilt an ageing system into a pipeline that worked this way.
    Explorations came back as working components, and approved decisions moved
    toward engineering as code instead of as redlines someone had to interpret.
  </p>
  <p>It paid off on both sides of the handoff:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Designers could explore without abandoning product coherence.</li>
    <li>Engineers got behaviour as code, not as a description of code.</li>
    <li>Accessibility and brand rules were present before review, not after it.</li>
    <li>A new decision could strengthen the shared system instead of becoming an isolated exception.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/ops-manager">The product and the system it was built from</a> are both
    running, and the component library documents the states that prove it.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    A design system this close to production decides what the product is allowed
    to become.
  </p>
  <h2>Options come back as working software</h2>
  <p>
    Making three versions of something is cheap now. Choosing between them is
    the part that still costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a recent engagement I directed three approaches to the same problem and
    deployed each to its own URL. We evaluated actual behavior, content,
    responsiveness and edge cases rather than debating static approximations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Running software changes the conversation. A stakeholder can try the
    workflow. An engineer can see a structural problem. A designer can compare
    hierarchy under real content. The team can discover that two people read the
    same requirement differently — before either reading hardens into production
    work.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also surfaces what a specification hides. A designer may use
    a word differently from an engineer. A product manager may assume an edge
    case is included. An agent may satisfy the literal instruction and miss the
    reason behind it. Running software makes those misunderstandings observable
    while they are still cheap.
  </p>
  <p>
    I still read every option, rank them, and say why. That is the part of the
    job the tooling has not touched.
  </p>


  <p>
    None of this is a workaround for unreliable AI. Talking it through has always
    been how a hard product decision becomes a shared one. What is different is
    that the thing being discussed shows up sooner.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/review-loop">A stakeholder review run this way</a> is on the site: two
    running builds, forty-eight notes, and the three that show where the human
    work actually was.
  </p>
  <h2>How I read a generated screen</h2>
  <p>A generated screen can be clean, consistent, and still wrong.</p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      lenses.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    This is where design leadership stays close to the work. I do not need to
    draw every artifact by hand to hold a bar, but I do need to recognise where
    the work is coherent, where it is merely plausible, and where the system
    underneath it needs to improve.
  </p>
  <h2>What the agent may not decide</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can run checks, implement approved patterns, generate options inside
    the system, and propose an addition when no pattern fits. Three limits are
    worth stating, and they are the places where it would otherwise make a design
    decision by default.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      boundaries.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    None of this assumes the agent behaves badly; it exists because speed
    magnifies misunderstandings. What I want is useful autonomy, with visible
    consequences and clear accountability.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anything that ships still belongs to people. Whoever approves the
    work needs to understand what changed, which checks ran, where uncertainty
    remains, and what could be affected. A human name on a change should mean
    informed responsibility, not a ritual click after an automated process.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same idea runs through my product work.
    In <a class="link" href="/alucify">Alucify</a> I designed the surfaces that expose coverage gaps,
    downstream impact, stale analysis, and the places where human interpretation
    is still required.
  </p>
  <p>
    I do not ask whether an agent is trustworthy in the abstract. I ask whether
    this result is grounded, reviewable, and safe to advance.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    I automate execution more readily than I automate judgment.
  </p>
  <h2>What changed is the material</h2>
  <p>
    Design leadership used to scale mostly through capacity: how much a team
    could research, draw, review, document and shepherd through implementation.
    Capacity still matters, but it is no longer the only constraint. The brief,
    the system, the loop and the perimeter are the other one, and they let me stay
    close to consequential decisions without becoming the production bottleneck.
  </p>
  <p>
    I am not presenting myself as a frontend engineer, and I do not think every
    designer needs to become one. I am describing a change in the material of
    design. Less gets lost between the people who decide something and the people
    who build it, and the opportunity is to remove enough production friction that
    designers spend more of their time understanding problems, comparing
    possibilities, and improving what shapes the next piece of work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tools are new. What the work has to be has not changed: useful, coherent,
    accessible, explainable, and right for the people it serves.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    My leverage is the system the work comes out of.
  </p>

</DocumentLayout>
